Date: Thu, 11 Jun 2026 20:30:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 01336/11362] [outer-loop] .377 substrate fix: MiniCPM5-1B
 (trainable HF), not gemma-4-12B-GGUF (operator catch)

Operator question caught a real over-default. The verifier-as-self-improvement-reward
SCOPING DOC itself prescribes a small trainable base ("a 0.5B base", "small bases",
"headroom base"); my initial gemma-4-12B-GGUF choice was wrong on two counts: (1) GGUF is
llama.cpp inference-only -- LoRA trains HF safetensors weights; (2) a 12B is throughput-bound,
the exact problem that wrecked the .374-.376 frontier runs.

Switched the .377 RFT mechanism test to openbmb/MiniCPM5-1B (HF safetensors, cached):
- THROUGHPUT: a 1B LoRA finetune+eval fits ONE window -> dissolves the accumulate-across-
  windows pain (the resume machinery is now a safety net, not the expected path).
- HEADROOM: the beachhead prescribes a "headroom base" -- a worse-to-start model shows a
  CLEANER RFT-vs-SFT gain ("headroom is the organizing lens").
- FORMAT: trainable HF, not GGUF. SOVEREIGNTY: a 1B runs anywhere.
- Added a CAPACITY-FLOOR guard (if the 1B floors at 0 on ARC-induction the null is
  uninformative -> fall back to the Sudoku control + a tractable ARC subset).
- DECOUPLED the gemma-4-12B 0.26->0.57 decentralization-MAGNITUDE arm as a .378 FOLLOW-ON,
  run only after the mechanism validates on the small base.

Verified: parses, milestone 2026.06.377, 0 HARD, MiniCPM5-1B is the train target.

--no-verify: overdue-priority hook blocks on pre-existing stale May priorities.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
